WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.410
MARKUS KLUTE: Let's
come back to 8.701.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.780
So in the previous
video, we talked

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.510
about higher-order
diagrams and we

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.110
looked at how we can
classify those contributions

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.850
to the total matrix element.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.490
We didn't do any of
the calculations,

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.410
or we didn't calculate the
Feynman diagram itself.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.830
And I'm not actually planning
to do this in the lecture.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.800
What we want to do
here is investigate

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.650
one of the features, a very
important features of having

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.140
those higher-order corrections.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.360
So if we look at this
higher-order diagram here,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.040
one of the specific
ones where we

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.330
have a self correction,
self energy correction

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.450
to the propagator C here.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.220
We find this loop
here in the middle.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.840
And if you were to-- and we
have all the tools at hand

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.490
to actually do the
calculation-- if you

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:58.200
were to calculate the amplitude,
we find this term here.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:58.830
So very good.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.990
So let's investigate.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.000
The first part is this
finite element here,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.590
which we can rewrite
as q cubed times

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.130
a finite element dq times
all the angles which

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.390
we have to integrate around.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:16.120
So we find this q cubed.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.600
If you look at under
in this fraction here,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.580
we find a q squared
times q squared.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.950
And if you go to just
very large values of q,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.840
that's the only
thing which remains.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.550
So we have an integral, 1
over q to the fourth power,

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.590
times q to the third power.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:39.350
And then we have to integrate
this from 0 to infinity.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.680
So if you do this,
you know that this

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.400
results in a logarithmic term.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.120
And if you have this
evaluated at infinite,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.530
we find that it diverges.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:53.010
So the result of the
integral is infinity.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.850
That is a real problem.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.840
If you calculate the
scattering process,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.850
the result is
better not infinite.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.590
The cross-section
shouldn't be infinite.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.970
The lifetime shouldn't be 0.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.350
So that is a real problem.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.570
And that actually caused
this entire theory

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.270
to not really make much
progress for quite some time,

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.110
because you were not actually
able to calculate anything.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.990
The solution is to
introduce a cutoff.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:23.460
So what happens now if
we don't just [? jump ?]

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.580
to the integration
[INAUDIBLE],, but to some scale.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:34.772
And so you introduce this
additional factor here

00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:36.240
in the integral.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.510
And you just calculate
the integral up

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.280
to a cutoff scale m.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.840
And then you have
an additional term

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.960
you have to in principle
evaluate from m to infinite.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.740
And you find that that
additional element is still

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:50.550
infinite.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.270
You can evaluate
all the other parts.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.360
And it turns out if you
are smart and introduce

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.500
the cutoff, the theory,
the calculation still

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.040
remain sensible, meaning
that they perform fine

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.690
under Lorentz transformation.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.620
All the physics intuition
we have is fine.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.900
You just have the
issue that still there

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.440
is a contribution to this
integral, which is infinite.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:20.160
It turns out now by miracle
that you can redefine, re-scale,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.850
or re-normalize the physical
objects in your calculation

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.675
such that it appears that there
is a correction to your masses

00:03:30.675 --> 00:03:33.960
or a correction
to your couplings.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.470
So what you find
then is that there

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.140
is a component which is
your physical value, which

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.080
is a bare mass and the bare
coupling, your coupling

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.390
constant, plus some correction.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:49.730
There's still a problem
that those corrections

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:54.020
at infinite scale are infinite.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.740
However, when we
do experiments, we

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.960
are performing them
at a specific scale.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.570
And so this problem of if
you go to really high scales,

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.380
things get out of hand, is
actually not a real problem

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.990
when you compare the theoretical
prediction with the experiment.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.000
There's an interesting
feature here.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.810
When you actually look in
the running or the evolution

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.690
of your coupling,
which is shown here,

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.550
the function of
energy that shows this

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:28.750
as a logarithm of
the energy, you

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.750
can do this for the
electromagnetic, for the weak,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.160
and for strong interactions.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.360
And note here that this is
an inverse of the coupling.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:38.830
They all run.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.140
They all are dependent
and have to be

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.260
evaluated at a specific scale.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.570
But unfortunately at
very high mass scale,

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:51.760
they don't all appear to
converge in the same spot.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.160
It is interesting to
know that if I introduce

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.020
new particles along
the scale here,

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.640
note that this is 10 to 10
GeV, this new particle will

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.810
change the behavior of the
running of the couplings.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.660
The energy behavior
of the coupling

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.670
changes if I introduce
new couplings.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.830
And you can already
understand this

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.380
because I would introduce
new diagrams which

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.340
contribute in this way.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.300
And then those
result in a change

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.130
in the running behavior
of the [? plot. ?]

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.160
So one of the ideas
for new physics,

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.860
which we might discuss in
the very last lecture of this

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:31.210
class, is that by introducing
new particles along the way,

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.930
you are actually able to combine
all of the couplings involved--

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.060
here, electromagnetic,
weak, and strong--

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.190
at a specific and
specific scale,

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.880
and then have a combined,
unified theory describing

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.982
all of the physics we discuss
in nuclear and particle physics.

00:05:47.982 --> 00:05:48.940
So that would be great.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.580
That is new physics, and we
don't know if this is realized.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.775
However, what is realized
in our calculations

00:05:54.775 --> 00:05:59.740
is that the physical masses
and couplings we observe,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.700
they are evaluated
at a specific scale.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.395
And they do run as function of
a scale as shown in this plot

00:06:07.395 --> 00:06:08.380
here.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.210
We will look at this
very specifically

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:15.290
at the running of those
three interactions--

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.680
the electromagnetic, the
weak, and the strong.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.480
And we can also
observe this when

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.220
we study the masses involved.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:26.710
They're have to be evaluated
or are evaluated in experiments

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.580
at a specific scale.

